  "bloques": [
    {
      "tipo": "explicacion",
      "titulo": {
        "en": "What happened to you"
      },
      "texto": {
        "en": "Many common injury expressions use a pronominal verb and a pronoun for the affected person. The body part often takes the definite article rather than a possessive: **me he roto el brazo**, **me he cortado el dedo**, **me quemé la mano**, **me torcí el tobillo**, **me di un golpe en la cabeza**. The pronoun already identifies who is affected. An indefinite article is also possible when introducing an unspecified injury: **me he roto una pierna**.\n\n**Caerse** means to fall: **me caí** por la escalera; **se cayó** de la bici.\n\nA story can set the scene with the imperfect and then report the accident: **estaba cocinando y me quemé**; **iba** en bici y **me caí**.\n\nPossible injuries include **una herida**, **un corte**, **una quemadura** and **un golpe**. For care, you may hear **una tirita**, **una venda**, **hielo**, **una escayola / un yeso**; someone may **ponerte una escayola** or **darte puntos**. **Tirita** is marked as Spanish in the DLE; **curita** and **apósito** are also used.\n\nTo ask what happened or how someone feels: **¿Qué te ha pasado?**, **¿Te has hecho daño?**, **¿Te duele?**"
      }
    },
